Adaptations replace the original CLI download and embedded Agent flow with the
installed video-creation application, preserve revision-aware editing, and defer
media service access and paid requests to the platform's existing authorization.
Role, standing instructions, local CLI locator, tests and integration guide are
maintained by desirecore-agent contributors under the repository MIT License.

OpenFilm's name in the source references and copyright notices is retained for
accurate attribution. The independent product is named 视频创作; its associated
Agent is named 视频导演.

General-motion extensions:

The parameter/data/track/event collaboration guidance, original-scene workflow,
local SDK integration instructions, wrapper argument separator and related tests
are independently maintained in this repository. Existing MIT OpenFilm creative
skills were adapted for narration-optional product demos, data explainers, music
visuals and whiteboard explanations; their original texts remain preserved.

The adapted motion skill now refers to the existing scene-read/scene-revise
contract. Narration guidance distinguishes measured, estimated and unknown timing,
preserves the meaning of human locks, and requires actual listening for audio claims.
The long-recording and presenter guidance now permits traceable SDR working media,
preserves source audio-time origins, and distinguishes subtitle edits from planned
media cuts guarded by film and subtitle revisions. The optional sentence-cut and
preview guidance reuses the application's timeline rather than prescribing a
creative workflow. These changes are independently maintained; the preserved
upstream texts are unchanged.
Write-request guidance also exposes complete parameters and baselines while retaining the same approval boundary, including supported standard input.
Safe-spacing and optional semantic subtitle-emphasis guidance are independently maintained and preserve human locks and the unchanged upstream references.
Portrait information-safe-zone guidance uses relative frame dimensions and preserves creative choice, explicit user constraints and human locks.
Active purpose-based review guidance selects observations from the current risks, checks covered film/subtitle layers, and reuses short-segment and comparison capabilities without prescribing a creative workflow.
Effective-layout, cue-audio bound to observed versions and media bytes, and existing-review recovery guidance reuses the application contracts without adding an audio service or changing upstream references.

